      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Dead Hangs and Your Spine: What Hanging Can and Cannot Do</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No trial shows that dead hangs treat back pain or repair discs. Some conditions need medical advice first.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div>
      <div class="container">

        <h2>What Hanging Does to the Spine</h2>
        <p>Hanging from a bar removes the compressive load that standing and sitting place on the spine while you hang. People are a little shorter in the evening than in the morning. A hang is one way to take load off the spine during the day.</p>
        <p>With a grip on the bar, gravity pulls the torso, pelvis and legs downward. This creates traction along the spinal column. The intervertebral discs act as shock absorbers between the vertebrae. They lose and regain fluid with changes in load.</p>
        <p>No study has measured disc height or disc pressure during a bodyweight dead hang. No study has shown that a hang changes disc shape or height in a lasting way.</p>

        <h2>What the Research on Spinal Traction Shows</h2>
        <p>Clinical traction applies axial force to separate vertebrae under controlled conditions. It can widen the spaces between vertebrae while the force is applied. A dead hang uses your own bodyweight as the load. This is a different and uncontrolled intervention.</p>
        <p>A Cochrane review of traction for low back pain (Wegner et al. 2013) found that traction has little or no effect on pain, function or return to work. No study shows a lasting effect from dead hangs on the spine.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hangs and Lower Back Stiffness</h2>
        <p>Most adults have low back pain at some point. Estimates vary with how it is defined. Some people find short hangs ease stiffness. Relief is not guaranteed. No trial gives a dose or a timeline for hanging and back stiffness.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Common Hang Routine for Back Stiffness</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Hang for 15 to 30 seconds per set.</li>
            <li>Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
            <li>Complete 3 to 4 sets per session.</li>
            <li>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days.</li>
            <li>Stop if symptoms worsen. See a clinician if stiffness has not eased.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>This back stiffness routine is common training practice, not a tested prescription. Comfortable short hangs are easier to repeat than long ones.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hangs and Herniated Discs</h2>
        <p>Disc herniations range from mild bulges to severe extrusions that compress nerve roots. No evidence shows that dead hangs help a herniated disc. Some people find that traction aggravates their specific disc problem.</p>
        <p>Severe herniations with significant nerve compression are a contraindication for dead hangs. Symptoms like leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder control need immediate medical attention.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Important Medical Notice</h3>
          <p>Ask a physician or physiotherapist before using dead hangs with a diagnosed herniated disc. Imaging results help a clinician judge whether traction is appropriate for your condition. Self-treating a severe herniation may worsen nerve compression.</p>
        </div>

        <p>If your clinician approves hanging then keep your feet on the ground so the bar carries only part of your weight. Monitor symptoms during and after each session. Any increase in radiating leg pain means you should stop and reassess with your provider.</p>

        <h2>Hang Dosage for Back Stiffness</h2>
        <p>Comfortable short hangs are easier to repeat than long ones. Consistency matters more than duration. The table shows example dosage by level. It is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
          <caption>Example hang dosage by level (practical guidance, not trial-tested)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Level</th>
              <th scope="col">Duration Per Set</th>
              <th scope="col">Sets</th>
              <th scope="col">Frequency</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Beginner</td>
              <td>10 to 15 seconds</td>
              <td>3</td>
              <td>3 days a week, non-consecutive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Intermediate</td>
              <td>20 to 30 seconds</td>
              <td>3 to 4</td>
              <td>3 days a week, non-consecutive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Advanced</td>
              <td>30 to 60 seconds</td>
              <td>3 to 4</td>
              <td>3 days a week, more only if pain free</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <p>Accumulating 60 to 90 seconds of total hang time per session is a common target. Split it into sets that match your grip capacity. Grip strength often limits hang duration first. A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway pull-up bar</a> at home makes it easy to fit a hang into the day.</p>

        <h2>Complementary Stretches</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs unload the spine along its length. Many people pair them with stretches for the muscles around the hips and lower back. These muscles can contribute to a feeling of tightness.</p>

        <h3>Cat-Cow</h3>
        <p>Start on hands and knees. Arch your back upward like an angry cat. Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Drop your belly toward the floor and lift your head. Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Alternate for 10 to 15 repetitions. Cat-cow moves the thoracic and lumbar segments through flexion and extension.</p>

        <h3>Child's Pose</h3>
        <p>Kneel and sit your hips back toward your heels. Extend your arms forward along the floor. Hold for 30 to 60 seconds. Child's pose stretches the lumbar erectors and latissimus dorsi. Many people do it right after their hang sets.</p>

        <h3>Hip Flexor Stretch</h3>
        <p>Tight hip flexors can pull the lumbar spine into extra arching. Kneel on one knee with the other foot forward. Push your hips forward until you feel a stretch in the front of the rear hip. Hold 30 to 45 seconds per side.</p>

        <h3>Knee-to-Chest Stretch</h3>
        <p>Lie on your back. Pull one knee toward your chest with both hands. Hold 20 to 30 seconds per side. The knee-to-chest stretch works the gluteals and lower back muscles.</p>

        <h2>When Dead Hangs Won't Help</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs are not appropriate for every back condition. Certain conditions need different treatment. Hanging can make an existing problem worse.</p>

        <h3>Acute Injury</h3>
        <p>Do not hang during a flare-up of acute back pain. Wait for it to settle and ask a clinician if unsure.</p>

        <h3>Severe Herniation</h3>
        <p>Large herniations that compress the spinal cord or cause significant nerve deficits are not candidates for self-directed traction. Bilateral leg weakness, saddle numbness or bladder dysfunction require urgent medical evaluation. Dead hangs cannot address these conditions.</p>

        <h3>Spinal Stenosis</h3>
        <p>Spinal stenosis narrows the spinal canal through bony changes. Stenosis symptoms often ease in forward bending and worsen in backward bending. Ask your clinician whether hanging suits your stenosis.</p>

        <h3>Post-Surgical</h3>
        <p>Spinal surgery changes the structure of the operated segments. Hanging applies traction through the surgical site. Follow your surgeon's timeline for returning to hanging.</p>

        <p>The <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> covers more precautions for people with existing conditions.</p>

        <h2>Getting Started Safely</h2>
        <p>Going straight to a full dead hang puts sudden traction through an unprepared spine. Building up in stages gives tissues time to adapt. The stages below are common training practice. They are not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Gradual Progression for Back Stiffness</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stage 1:</strong> Stand under the bar and grip it with both feet flat on the ground. Bend your knees slightly so your arms carry part of your bodyweight. Hold 15 to 20 seconds for 3 sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 2:</strong> Lift one foot off the ground at a time and alternate feet between sets. Hold 15 to 20 seconds for 3 sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 3:</strong> Lift both feet off the ground for a full dead hang. Start with 10 to 15 second holds and add time as your grip allows.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 4:</strong> Maintain 3 to 4 sets of 20 to 30 seconds in 3 sessions a week.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Stop at any sign of sharp pain. Some dull aching or mild discomfort can occur while adapting. Sharp, shooting or radiating pain means the load is too high. Reduce the weight your arms carry or the duration and try again.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">dead hang form guide</a> covers grip placement and breathing.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long should I dead hang for back stiffness?</h3>
          <p>Common practice is 15 to 30 second hangs for 3 to 4 sets. This adds up to about 60 to 90 seconds of hanging per session on 3 non-consecutive days a week. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription. No trial has tested a dose for back stiffness. Stop if symptoms worsen. See a clinician if stiffness has not eased.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can dead hangs help a herniated disc?</h3>
          <p>No evidence shows that dead hangs help a herniated disc. See a physician or physiotherapist before hanging with a diagnosed herniation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are dead hangs better than inversion tables for back pain?</h3>
          <p>Dead hangs and inversion tables both apply traction by different methods. No head-to-head trial was found. People with high blood pressure or eye conditions should ask a clinician before inverting. Inversion tables may suit people who cannot grip a bar.</p>
        </div>

